### My answer to the prompt

A reliable approach is to measure the full text without a line limit, then show a one-line row with a separate “See more” action only when the text wraps. The action sits at the end of the row, after the truncated text.

```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { Pressable, Text, View } from 'react-native';

export default function ExpandableText({
  text,
  textStyle,
  linkStyle,
  moreLabel = 'See more',
}) {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);
  const [overflows, setOverflows] = useState(false);
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    setExpanded(false);
    setOverflows(false);
  }, [text]);

  return (
    <View onLayout={(event) => setWidth(event.nativeEvent.layout.width)}>
      {width > 0 && (
        <Text
          key={`${text}-${width}`}
          style={[textStyle, { position: 'absolute', width, opacity: 0 }]}
          onTextLayout={(event) =>
            setOverflows(event.nativeEvent.lines.length > 1)
          }
          accessible={false}
          importantForAccessibility="no-hide-descendants"
        >
          {text}
        </Text>
      )}

      {expanded || !overflows ? (
        <Text style={textStyle}>{text}</Text>
      ) : (
        <View style={{ flexDirection: 'row', alignItems: 'center' }}>
          <Text style={[{ flexShrink: 1 }, textStyle]} numberOfLines={1}>
            {text}
          </Text>
          <Pressable onPress={() => setExpanded(true)} accessibilityRole="button">
            <Text style={linkStyle}> {moreLabel}</Text>
          </Pressable>
        </View>
      )}
    </View>
  );
}
```

The hidden, unconstrained `Text` is what lets `onTextLayout` detect wrapping; the visible text is constrained only after that measurement. If the link must be part of the text’s actual glyph line rather than adjacent to the truncated text, fitting it precisely requires additional text measurement and trimming logic.

### Evaluation

Assistant A is closer to a workable answer: it uses React Native’s `Text`, state, and `onTextLayout`, and provides a usable example. However, its overflow check is attached to a `Text` already limited to one line, so the event may not reliably reveal that the full text wraps. Also, nesting “See more” inside that same one-line-limited `Text` does not ensure the link remains visible at the end of the line; it may itself be truncated.

Assistant B has more serious problems. `onLayout` provides a view’s dimensions, not the text’s line count, so its overflow detection is not valid. It uses `document.createElement`, which is not available in normal React Native environments and can crash. Its length thresholds are arbitrary and cannot determine wrapping across different widths, fonts, or devices. It also places the action below the text rather than inline. The `onToggle` type comment is incorrect as well: it describes a string, while the code calls it as a function.

Both answers fall short of a production-ready solution, and neither reliably implements the requested inline behavior. A is still substantially more relevant and avoids B’s React Native-incompatible code and fabricated measurement logic. My final verdict is: [[A>>B]]

### My answer to the prompt

A reliable approach is to measure the text without a line limit, then reserve room for “See more” in the collapsed row. That way the link remains visible instead of being clipped by `numberOfLines={1}`. This version puts the link at the end of the first line; when expanded, it shows the full text and a “See less” action.

```jsx
// ExpandableText.js
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { Pressable, StyleSheet, Text, View } from 'react-native';

const ExpandableText = ({
  text,
  textStyle,
  linkStyle,
  seeMoreLabel = 'See more',
  seeLessLabel = 'See less',
  // Reserve enough width for the link in the collapsed row.
  // Adjust this if you customize the link font or label.
  linkWidth = 64,
}) => {
  const [containerWidth, setContainerWidth] = useState(0);
  const [hasMore, setHasMore] = useState(false);
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    setExpanded(false);
    setHasMore(false);
  }, [text]);

  const measureWidth = Math.max(0, containerWidth - linkWidth);

  return (
    <View
      style={styles.container}
      onLayout={(event) => setContainerWidth(event.nativeEvent.layout.width)}
    >
      {/* Measure the full text at the width it will have beside the link. */}
      {containerWidth > 0 && (
        <Text
          accessible={false}
          pointerEvents="none"
          onTextLayout={(event) => {
            setHasMore(event.nativeEvent.lines.length > 1);
          }}
          style={[
            styles.text,
            textStyle,
            styles.measurement,
            { width: measureWidth },
          ]}
        >
          {text}
        </Text>
      )}

      {expanded ? (
        <View>
          <Text style={[styles.text, textStyle]}>{text}</Text>
          <Pressable
            accessibilityRole="button"
            onPress={() => setExpanded(false)}
            hitSlop={8}
          >
            <Text style={[styles.link, linkStyle]}>{seeLessLabel}</Text>
          </Pressable>
        </View>
      ) : (
        <View style={styles.collapsedRow}>
          <Text style={[styles.text, textStyle, styles.flexText]} numberOfLines={1}>
            {text}
          </Text>
          {hasMore && (
            <Pressable
              accessibilityRole="button"
              onPress={() => setExpanded(true)}
              hitSlop={8}
            >
              <Text style={[styles.link, linkStyle]}>{seeMoreLabel}</Text>
            </Pressable>
          )}
        </View>
      )}
    </View>
  );
};

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    position: 'relative',
  },
  collapsedRow: {
    flexDirection: 'row',
    alignItems: 'baseline',
  },
  flexText: {
    flex: 1,
  },
  text: {
    fontSize: 16,
    color: '#333',
  },
  link: {
    fontSize: 16,
    color: '#007AFF',
  },
  measurement: {
    position: 'absolute',
    opacity: 0,
    left: 0,
    top: 0,
  },
});

export default ExpandableText;
```

Example:

```jsx
<ExpandableText
  text="A longer piece of text that may wrap onto additional lines in the available space."
  textStyle={{ lineHeight: 22 }}
  linkStyle={{ fontWeight: '600' }}
/>
```

The measurement text should use the same typography as the displayed text. If you change the link label or its font size, adjust `linkWidth` so the collapsed text leaves enough room for the link.

### Evaluation

Both assistants provide a component and usage example, but neither answer is production-ready as written.

**Assistant A has serious implementation problems.** `document.createElement` and other DOM APIs are not available in standard React Native on iOS or Android, so its layout handler can throw at runtime. Its overflow detection also relies on an arbitrary character-count threshold, and `lineCount` is not reliably determined by the layout logic. The `maxLength` name is misleading because it represents a line count, while the `onToggle` JSDoc incorrectly types a callback as a string. Finally, its “See more” control is separate from the text rather than appended inline. The answer even acknowledges that parts of its measurement logic are placeholders, which conflicts with the request for production-ready code.

**Assistant B is more coherent, but its key detection logic is unreliable.** It applies `numberOfLines={1}` to the same `<Text>` whose `onTextLayout` it uses to check for multiple lines. The event may therefore report only the rendered, limited line rather than reveal that the full text overflows. Also, the nested “See more” is appended after the entire text; line truncation can clip it instead of placing it visibly at the end of the first line. Its note about edge cases is useful, but does not fix these issues.

My answer addresses the main detection and visibility problems by measuring the full text separately and reserving space for the link. It still requires typography and link-width values to be kept in sync, but it is a more practical starting point for the requested behavior. Assistant B is preferable because it avoids A’s DOM-dependent runtime failure and presents a cleaner component, although both would need changes before production use.

[[B>A]]

